class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> leftView(Node root) {
    
        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();                          // Initialize result list
        Queue<Node> q = new LinkedList<>();                                   // Queue for BFS traversal
        
        if(root == null) return list;                                         // Handle empty tree
        
        q.add(root);                                                          // Enqueue root
        
        while(!q.isEmpty()){                                                  // Process until queue empty
            int size = q.size();                                              // Nodes at current level
            
            for(int i=0;i<size;i++){
                Node cur = q.remove();                                        // Dequeue current node
                
                if(i == 0){ list.add(cur.data); }                             // First node of level = leftmost
                
                if(cur.left != null) q.add(cur.left);                         // Enqueue left child
                if(cur.right != null) q.add(cur.right);                       // Enqueue right child
            }
        }
        return list;                                                          // Return left view
    }
}

//////////////////////// DRY RUN /////////////////////////////

Example: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, N, N]

      1
     / \
    2   3
   / \
  4   5

Iteration 1: size=1, i=0 → add 1 (leftmost), add 2, 3
Queue: [2, 3]
Iteration 2: size=2, i=0 → add 2 (leftmost), add 4, 5
Queue: [3, 4, 5]
Iteration 3: size=3, i=0 → add 4 (leftmost), add nothing
Queue: [5]
Iteration 4: size=1, i=0 → add 5 (leftmost), add nothing
Queue: []

Output: [1, 2, 4, 5]

//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Left View of Binary Tree
Given the root of a binary tree, return its left view.
The left view is the set of nodes visible when viewed from the left side.

Note: If the tree is empty, return an empty list.

Examples:

------------------------------------------------------------------

Input: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, N, N]

      1
     / \
    2   3
   / \
  4   5

Output: [1, 2, 4]
Explanation: Leftmost nodes at each level: 1, 2, 4

------------------------------------------------------------------

Input: root = [1, 2, 3, N, N, 4, N, N, 5, N, N]

      1
     / \
    2   3
   /
  4
   \
    5

Output: [1, 2, 4, 5]
Explanation: Leftmost nodes at each level: 1, 2, 4, 5

------------------------------------------------------------------

Constraints:
• 0 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 10^5
• 0 ≤ node->data ≤ 10^5
